<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The launcher shortcuts: long-press the icon and land on a screen.

  WHY THESE EXIST. The choose screen is a menu, and a menu is what an OS can
  render better than an app can - it is already on screen, already under the
  thumb, and it costs no launch. So the two things qrdrop does are offered
  before it starts, and a person who wanted the scanner does not open the app
  in order to say so a second time.

  HOW THE INTENT REACHES THE WEB LAYER. The extra below is read by
  MainActivity.stashLaunchAction, written to a small JSON in the app cache
  directory, taken and deleted by src/share.rs's take_launch_action, and handed
  to the component's startAction by app/src/main.js. That is exactly the chain
  the share sheet already uses, with an action string where the share has a
  file - one mechanism, documented in one place, rather than a second way of
  getting an intention across the same boundary.

  The order is deliberate: scan first. It is the shortcut that saves the most,
  since sending already begins in a file picker the OS draws, and a launcher
  that shows only one is showing the more useful one.

  If a launcher ever failed to inflate the extra, the shortcut would open the
  app on the choose screen - which is what tapping the icon does. The failure
  mode is losing the shortcut's value, never doing the wrong thing.
-->
<shortcuts xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="scan"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_qrdrop_scan"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_scan_long">
        <intent
            android:action="android.intent.action.MAIN"
            android:targetPackage="com.stan_ely.qrdrop"
            android:targetClass="com.stan_ely.qrdrop.MainActivity">
            <extra
                android:name="com.stan_ely.qrdrop.LAUNCH_ACTION"
                android:value="receive" />
        </intent>
    </shortcut>

    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="send"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_qrdrop_send"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_send_short"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_send_long">
        <intent
            android:action="android.intent.action.MAIN"
            android:targetPackage="com.stan_ely.qrdrop"
            android:targetClass="com.stan_ely.qrdrop.MainActivity">
            <extra
                android:name="com.stan_ely.qrdrop.LAUNCH_ACTION"
                android:value="send" />
        </intent>
    </shortcut>
</shortcuts>
